## Tool Name
`@agent-tools/graph`

## Description
A general-purpose graph data structure package providing directed, undirected, and mixed graphs with built-in traversal, shortest-path, community detection, and topological sort algorithms — enabling agents to model and reason about relationships, dependencies, and networks.

## Why It's Useful for Agents
Agents frequently need to model relationships: task dependency ordering, knowledge graphs, call graphs, entity relationship mapping, workflow DAGs, and impact analysis. Currently there's no agent-tools package for general graph operations beyond the built-in dependency graph (issue #15, which was narrowly scoped to dependency ordering).

Based on research into [graphology](https://github.com/graphology/graphology) (1.6k stars, MIT, TypeScript, v0.26.0 Feb 2025) — a robust multipurpose graph library with a comprehensive standard library including:
- **Traversal**: BFS, DFS
- **Shortest path**: Dijkstra, A*
- **Community detection**: Louvain method
- **Components**: connected/strongly-connected component decomposition
- **DAG utilities**: cycle detection, topological sort
- **Centrality/metrics**: modularity, density, betweenness
- **Operators**: union, intersection, reverse, conversion
- **Generators**: random, complete, lattice graphs
- **Serialization**: GEXF, GraphML import/export

Also evaluated [@dagrejs/graphlib](https://github.com/dagrejs/graphlib) (1.7k stars, MIT, TypeScript) — solid for directed multigraphs but narrower algorithm set and less active ecosystem.

## Proposed API
```typescript
import { createGraph, algorithms } from "@agent-tools/graph";

// Create a directed graph
const g = createGraph({ type: "directed" });

// Add nodes and edges with metadata
g.addNode("taskA", { label: "Build", duration: 30 });
g.addNode("taskB", { label: "Test", duration: 15 });
g.addEdge("taskA", "taskB", { weight: 1 });

// Traversal
algorithms.bfs(g, "taskA", (node, attrs) => { /* visit */ });
algorithms.dfs(g, "taskA", (node, attrs) => { /* visit */ });

// Shortest path (Dijkstra)
const path = algorithms.shortestPath(g, "taskA", "taskD");
// => { path: ["taskA", "taskB", "taskD"], weight: 3 }

// Topological sort (DAG operations)
const order = algorithms.topoSort(g);
// => ["taskA", "taskB", "taskC", "taskD"]

// Cycle detection
const cycles = algorithms.detectCycles(g);
// => [] or [["A", "B", "C", "A"]]

// Connected components
const components = algorithms.connectedComponents(g);

// Community detection (Louvain)
const communities = algorithms.communities(g);

// Centrality metrics
const centrality = algorithms.betweenness(g);

// Serialization
const json = g.export(); // JSON-serializable
const restored = createGraph({ data: json });
```

## Scope
**In scope:**
- Directed, undirected, and mixed graph creation
- Node/edge CRUD with arbitrary metadata
- BFS/DFS traversal
- Shortest path (Dijkstra, A*)
- Topological sort and cycle detection
- Connected/strongly-connected components
- Community detection (Louvain)
- Centrality metrics (betweenness, closeness, degree)
- Graph operators (union, intersection, reverse)
- JSON serialization/deserialization

**Out of scope:**
- Visualization/rendering (use sigma.js or similar directly)
- Force-directed layouts (rendering concern)
- GEXF/GraphML file format support (niche, can be added later)
- Distributed/remote graph databases
